know your place be a shoe snowpiercer  again well I don't do straightforward  movie reviews on here largely just  because I rarely get to see stuff when  it first comes out but this one I do  want to talk about because it only had a  limited release and it's not going to be  available to see on disk until the end  of October and I want to get my thoughts  in before that happens now this is the  english-language debut of korean  director bong joon-ho who I know is the  director of the host no no no no no not  that one god no the other one the go yes  that one the monster one who so  snowpiercer set in the future and is  dealing with a post-apocalyptic scenario  where in an attempt to try and reverse  global warming humans inadvertently set  off a new ice age not just a regular ice  age like a mega ice age everything is  dead the only survivors are the  occupants of this train it's in  perpetual motion around the globe and  it's divided very rigidly by class the  most wretched people are stuck in the  back of the train and the people in  front and control obviously living a  much nicer life now the people stuck in  the back they have been putting up with  it for years and after years of  maltreatment they are ready to storm the  train take control of the engine this is  the kind of big concept sci-fi that  really doesn't get made all that much  anymore I think part of the problem with  studios tend to fear that audiences will  start poking holes in the basic premise  right off the bat but if you're doing  that you're questioning the logical or  scientific viability of the opening  premise of the film then you've missed  the point because the point is not the  train and the Ice Age these are just  concepts being used to explore bigger  questions of things like class warfare  societal obligation and human nature  these sorts of movies really have their  heyday in the 60s and 70s when sci-fi  concepts were used to explore social  situations before Star Wars came along  and every sci-fi movie since then has  just been about giving us explosions in  space  news and really whether or not you can  set that expectation aside is going to  be the key to whether or not you're  going to enjoy this film because having  been raised by and trained by American  movies there is a point about midway  through this movie that it takes a shift  and if you can't get your head out of  the American blockbuster mentality the  movie might well lose you because right  at the point where if this was an  American movie with an American director  made by an American studio the point at  which the thing would just step on the  gas and go full throttle to the climax  is exactly where it slows down and it  does that deliberately it does it with a  purpose to make sure you're paying  attention and you've got to be able to  make that gear shift with the movie so  that you can take in what it's doing  otherwise you're going to be sitting  there checking your watch waiting for  the next action scene now don't get me  wrong there are more action scenes later  on and they're really good they're well  constructed their great set pieces but  the action is not the point and the  movie is not going to let you get away  with sitting through it just to watch  the action scene if you can make that  shift there is a lot to enjoying soaked  in about this movie aside from just the  big concepts that it deals with them  dealing with them very interestingly and  very well it also offers something that  we so rarely see any more genuine cast  diversity okay yeah sure we've got  captain america chris evans in the lead  he does fantastic work for the record  though but we've also got front and  center main characters this korean  father and daughter who are speaking  Korean for most of the movie you've got  Octavia Spencer right up there and even  amongst the rest of the white guys we've  got diversity in terms of Jamie Bell  we've got John Hurt we've got Luke  Pasqualino you know there's this mix of  ethnicities and ages and agendas I mean  it's both refreshing unto itself it  helps with the notion of this really is  just a microcosm for the world and some  of the points that the film is trying to  make it helps make them all the better  as good as all those cast members who  have already talked about our though the  standout is Tilda Swinton she plays a  sort of middle management type and she  is she's almost impossible to describe  she is bizarre and over-the-top in a way  like is she do is this a joke is she a  caricature but there's actually a lot of  done a lot of layers me to give you an  idea of how ridiculous this woman is her  first big speech directed at the  occupants of the back of the Train she  compares them to a shoe and says that  their desire to move further up in the  cars and the train it's like putting a  shoe on your head I mean it's ridiculous  and it should break the movie but the  more you see her the more you sort of  see slightly behind this this facade of  insanity that's up there and you get the  depths of what she's I mean she she was  mesmerizing every second she was on the  screen if there's one complaint I might  have about this movie it might be that  it's trying to tackle a few too many  ideas there's some stuff that's just  kind of tacked on there with ideas about  indoctrination there's a little bit in  there about drug addiction that they  feel a little bit tacked on but  thankfully that not in a way that's  distracting they are there it doesn't  delve too much into them but they don't  distract from the main points that the  thing is making about human nature and  the role of authority and its bigger  concepts and the fact that these are  just kind of there but not delved into  overly actually kind of helps sell the  idea that this train is the world  trapped in a bottle and it brings all  the problems as i said the action scenes  are really good but and like I mentioned  you got to be sure that you can get out  of the normal blockbuster mindset  because this is not a standard  blockbuster it's got some blockbuster  beats and it's got some really  well-executed stuff if you go in there  wanting good actually you're going to  get good action but if that's all you're  going in for you are going to come away  confused and disappointed probably a  little annoyed but if you can just go  and be willing to travel to where this  movie wants to take you you're not going  to regret seeing it whether you agree  with the side that it takes on some of  these points or not it's just really  fascinating to see a movie this well  executed and dealing with these kinds of  questions now like I said this sort of  movie had its heyday back in the 60s and  70s so out of those sorts of  high-concept sci-fi films that tackle  social issues what was your favorite of  this kind of movie I'm talking about  things like Soylent Green silent running  Logan's Run the original Planet of the  Apes those sorts of films what  your personal favor whatever it is drop  something down in the comments and let's  talk about it until next time this  council is adjourned
 

